          %title Contents
          %blockquote
            %p
              This entry is about the classical Adams spectral sequence only. For more general discussion see at
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adams+spectral+sequence"} Adams spectral sequence
            %p under construction
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#MotivationFromHurewiczTheoremAndSerreSpectralSequence"} Via iterated Hurewicz theorem and Serre spectral sequence
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#generators_in_low_range"} Generators in low range
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#further"} Further
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em classical Adams spectral sequence
            (
            %a{:href => "#Adams58"}> Adams 58
            ) is a type of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+sequences"} spectral sequences
            used for computations in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups+of+spheres"} homotopy groups of spheres
            at prime 2 from
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"} homology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comodules"} comodules
            over its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steenrod+operations"} Steenrod operations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre+spectral+sequence"} Serre spectral sequence
            obtained by replacing a single fibration by an “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adams+resolution"}> Adams resolution
            ”.
          %p
            The original
            %em clasical Adams spectral sequenc
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            is further refined by the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adams-Novikov+spectral+sequence"} Adams-Novikov spectral sequence
            (
            %a{:href => "#Novikov67"}> Novikov 67
            ) by replacing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            modulo
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+cobordism+cohomology+theory"} complex cobordism cohomology theory
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown-Peterson+theory"} Brown-Peterson theory
            or the like.
          %p
            Generally, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            a suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-infinity+algebra"} E-infinity algebra
            there is a corresponding
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              \-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adams+spectral+sequence"} Adams spectral sequence
            whose second page is given by
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology"} generalized cohomology
            and which arises as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+sequence+of+a+simplicial+stable+homotopy+type"} spectral sequence of a simplicial stable homotopy type
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosimplicial+object"} cosimplicial
            object which is the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cech+nerve"} Cech nerve
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sweedler+coring"} Sweedler coring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Amitsur+complex"} Amitsur complex
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bousfield-Kan+spectral+sequence"} Bousfield-Kan
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+spectral+sequence"} homotopy spectral sequence
            in unstable
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %h3#MotivationFromHurewiczTheoremAndSerreSpectralSequence Via iterated Hurewicz theorem and Serre spectral sequence
          %p
            The classical Adams spectral sequence may be motivated from the strategy to compute
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+groups"} cohomology groups
            by subsequently applying the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurewicz+theorem"} Hurewicz theorem
            to compute the lowest-degree non-trivial homotopy group from the corresponding
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+group"} cohomology group
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber"} homotopy fiber
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre+spectral+sequence"} Serre spectral sequence
            to identify the next lowest non-trivial cohomology group of that fiber, and then iterating this process. The Adams spectral sequence arises when in this kind of strategy instead of co-killing only the lowest lying
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+group"} cohomology group
            %em all
            nontrivial cohomology groups, then forms the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber"} homotopy fiber
            and so on.
          %p
            This was apparently historically the way that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Adams"} John Adams
            indeed proceeded from
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Pierre+Serre"} Jean-Pierre Serre
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Wilson13"} Wilson 13, 1.1
          %p We now say this again in more detail.
          %p
            Given
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups+of+spheres"} homotopy groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi mod
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_k(S^n) \;mod \;2
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-sphere"} n-sphere
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \leq n
            this is clear: first for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \lt n
            they all vanish, and second for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = n
            we have, by the very nature of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+spaces"} Eilenberg-MacLane spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(\mathbb{Z}_2, n)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H^n(S^n, \mathbb{Z}_2) \simeq [S^n, K(\mathbb{Z}_2,n)] \simeq \pi_n(K(\mathbb{Z}_2,n)) \simeq \mathbb{Z}_2
          %p
            so that by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurewicz+theorem"} Hurewicz theorem
            it follows that also
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi mod
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pi_n(S^n) \;mod\;2 \;\simeq \mathbb{Z}_2
                  \\,.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurewicz+theorem"} Hurewicz theorem
            does not say anything beyond the first non-vanishing cohomology group, but so to apply it again we can move up one step in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+tower"} Whitehead tower
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n
            and hence consider the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber"} homotopy fiber
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mi n
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi c
                                %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mi K
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ,
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  F_1
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow
                  \\\
                  S^n &amp;\stackrel{c_1}{\longrightarrow}&amp; K(\mathbb{Z}_2,n)
                  }
          %p
            of the generator
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [c_1] = 1 \in \pi_n(S^n) \simeq \mathbb{Z}_2
          %p
            To apply the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurewicz+theorem"} Hurewicz theorem
            to that fiber we need to know its lowest non-trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+group"} cohomology group
            again, and this is computed via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre+spectral+sequence"} Serre spectral sequence
            applied to this
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+sequence"} fiber sequence
          %p
            From here on the process repeats, and one moves higher through the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+tower"} Whitehead tower
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi c
                                %mn 2
                        %mtd
                          %mi K
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ,
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mi n
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi c
                                %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mi K
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ,
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\vdots
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow
                  \\\
                  F_1 &amp;\stackrel{c_2}{\longrightarrow}&amp; K(\mathbb{Z}_2, n+1)
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow
                  \\\
                  S^n &amp;\stackrel{c_1}{\longrightarrow}&amp; K(\mathbb{Z}_2,n)
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            The Adams spectral sequence arises from this strategy by co-killing not just the first non-trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+group"} cohomology group
            at each stage, but
            %em all
            nontrivial cohomology groups at a given stage.
          %p
            This is done in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            (for instance the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere+spectrum"} sphere spectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mi 𝕊
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = \mathbb{S}
            if we still with the computation of the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+groups+of+spheres"} stable homotopy groups of spheres
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \mathbb{F}_2
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+spectrum"} Eilenberg-MacLane spectrum
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [c] \in H^n(X)
          %p
            is represented by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+class"} homotopy class
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectra"} spectra
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi n
                  %mi H
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  c \;\colon\;  X \longrightarrow \Sigma^n H\mathbb{F}_2
          %p
            (a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suspension"} suspension
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+type"} finite type
            then there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite"} finite
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            of non-trivial cohomology classes like this, and a choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_i
            for each of them gives a single map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi I
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ≔
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mi i
                  %mi H
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f_0
                  \\coloneqq
                  (c_i)_I \;\colon\;
                  X \longrightarrow K_0 \coloneqq \bigvee_{i \in I} \Sigma^{n_i}H \mathbb{F}_2
          %p
            into a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+Eilenberg-MacLane+spectrum"} generalized Eilenberg-MacLane spectrum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber"} homotopy fiber
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi f
                                %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi K
                            %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  F_1
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow
                  \\\
                  X &amp;\stackrel{f_0}{\longrightarrow}&amp; K_0
                  }
          %p
            which may be thought of as encoding all information about
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            beyond its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+groups"} cohomology groups
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+tower"} Whitehead tower
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adams+resolution"} Adams resolution
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mn 2
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi f
                                %mn 2
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi K
                            %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi f
                                %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi K
                            %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi f
                                %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi K
                            %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\vdots
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow
                  \\\
                  F_2 &amp;\stackrel{f_2}{\longrightarrow}&amp; K_2
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow
                  \\\
                  F_1 &amp;\stackrel{f_1}{\longrightarrow}&amp; K_1
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow
                  \\\
                  X &amp;\stackrel{f_0}{\longrightarrow}&amp; K_0
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            The
            %em Adams spectral sequence
            is that induced by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+couple"} exact couple
            obtained by applying
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_\bullet
            to this
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adams+resolution"} Adams resolution
          %p We now say this more in detail.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/long+exact+sequences+of+homotopy+groups"} long exact sequences of homotopy groups
            for all the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fibers"} homotopy fibers
            in this diagram arrange into a diagram of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↖
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mo •
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi π
                                %mo •
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %msub
                                %mi f
                                %mn 2
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mo •
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi K
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↖
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mo ∂
                                  %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mo •
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi π
                                %mo •
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %msub
                                %mi f
                                %mn 1
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mo •
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi K
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↖
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mo ∂
                                  %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mo •
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi π
                                %mo •
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %msub
                                %mi f
                                %mn 0
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mo •
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi K
                            %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\vdots
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp; \nwarrow
                  \\\
                  \\pi_\bullet(F_2) &amp;\stackrel{\pi_\bullet(f_2)}{\longrightarrow}&amp; \pi_\bullet(K_2)
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp; \nwarrow^{\mathrlap{\partial_2}}
                  \\\
                  \\pi_\bullet(F_1) &amp;\stackrel{\pi_\bullet(f_1)}{\longrightarrow}&amp; \pi_\bullet(K_1)
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp; \nwarrow^{\mathrlap{\partial_1}}
                  \\\
                  \\pi_\bullet(X) &amp;\stackrel{\pi_\bullet(f_0)}{\longrightarrow}&amp; \pi_\bullet(K_0)
                  }
                  \\,,
          %p
            where the diagonal maps are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connecting+homomorphisms"} connecting homomorphisms
            and hence decrease degree in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_\bullet
            by one. The idea now is to compute the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            from the decomposed information in this diagram as follows.
          %p
            First, by construction the homotopy groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_\bullet(K_s)
            are known, therefore we can identify elements
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\sigma \in \pi_\bullet(X)
          %p if they come from elements
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mi s
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\sigma_s \in \pi_\bullet(X_s)
          %p whose image
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pi_\bullet(f_s)(\sigma_s) \in \pi_\bullet(K_s)
          %p
            we understand. So the task is to understand the image of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_\bullet(f_s)
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_\bullet(K_s)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
          %p
            By
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequence"} exactness
            an element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi κ
                    %mi s
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa_s \in \pi_\bullet(K_s)
            is in this image if its image
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ρ
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo ∂
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi κ
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\rho_{s+1} \coloneqq \partial(\kappa_s) \in \pi_{\bullet-1}(X_{s+1})
          %p
            vanishes. Now, by construction of the resolution, “evidence” for this is that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∂
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi κ
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{s+1}(\partial(\kappa_s)) \in \pi_{\bullet-1}(K_{s+1})
            vanishes, which in turn by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequence"} exactness
            means equivalently that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∂
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi κ
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial(\kappa_s)
            is the image of an element
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ρ
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo +
                        %mn 2
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mrow
                        %mo •
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo +
                        %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mrow
                        %mo •
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_{s+2} \in  \pi_{\bullet-1}(X_{s+2}) \to \pi_{\bullet-1}(X_{s+1})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ρ
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_{s+2}
            to vanish is that its image
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{s+2}(\rho(s+2))
            vanishes, which again means that it comes from an element
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ρ
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo +
                        %mn 3
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mrow
                        %mo •
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo +
                        %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mrow
                        %mo •
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo +
                        %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_{s+3} \in  \pi_{\bullet-1}(X_{s+3}) \to \pi_{\bullet-1}(X_{s+2})
          %p
            Proceeding by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} induction
            this way, we find that accumulated “evidence” in homotopy groups of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_\bullet
            for an element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi κ
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa_s
            to represent an element in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_\bullet(X)
            is that its differential
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∂
                  %msub
                    %mi κ
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial \kappa_s
            factors through all the
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mrow
                        %mo •
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo +
                        %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mrow
                        %mo •
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_{\bullet-1}(X_{s+k}) \to \pi_{\bullet-1}(X_s)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+limit"} inverse limit
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %munder
                        %mi lim
                        %mo ←
                      %mi s
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mrow
                        %mo •
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{\leftarrow}{\lim}_s \pi_{\bullet-1}(X_s)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi κ
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa_s
            with
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∂
                  %msub
                    %mi κ
                    %mi s
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mo ←
                    %mi s
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\partial \kappa_s \in \underset{\leftarrow}{\lim}_s \pi_{\bullet-1}(X_s) \to \pi_{\bullet-1}(X_{s+1})
          %p
            is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %em permanent cycle
          %p
            In good cases, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adams+resolution"} Adams resolution
            is indeed a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resolution"} resolution
            which means that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+limit"} inverse limit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mo ←
                    %mi s
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{\leftarrow}{\lim}_s X_s
            is in fact
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible"} contractible
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mi s
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_s \in \pi_\bullet(X_s)
            and hence of an element
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \in \pi_\bullet(X)
          %p
            A trivial way for this to be the case is that the original
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_s
            is itself in the image under
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial
            of some element, in which case
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi κ
                    %mi s
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa_s = 0
            already all by itself. These elements are called
            = succeed "." do
              %em eventual boundaries
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi permanent
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi cycles
                    %mrow
                      %mi eventual
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi boundaries
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{permanent\;cycles}{eventual\;boundaries}
          %p
            gives elements in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_\bullet(S)
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#generators_in_low_range Generators in low range
          %p
            For the moment see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/May+spectral+sequence"} May spectral sequence
          %h3#further Further
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_j
            is a permanent cycle in the Adams spectral sequence if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mn 2
                        %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{(2^j)}
            admits the structure of a real
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division+algebra"} division algebra
          %p Adams: …
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mn 0
                  %msubsup
                    %mi h
                    %mi j
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d_2(h_{j+1}) = h_0 h_j^2
          %p
            Mahowald-Tangora:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi h
                    %mn 4
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_4^2
            is a permanent cycle
          %p
            Barratt-Jones-Mahowald:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi h
                    %mn 5
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_5^2
            is a permanent cycle
          %p
            Hill-Hopkins-Ravenel: for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi j
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 7
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j \gt 7
            then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi h
                    %mi j
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_j^2
            is not a permanent cycle.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/May+spectral+sequence"}> May spectral sequence
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Curtis+algorithm"} Curtis algorithm
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adams-Novikov+spectral+sequence"} Adams-Novikov spectral sequence
          %h2#references References
          %p The original article is
          %ul
            %li#Adams58
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frank+Adams"}> Frank Adams
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the structure and applications of the Steenrod algebra
          %p
            Textbook accounts proceeding in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coalgebra"} coalgebra
            picture include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Doug+Ravenel"}> Doug Ravenel
                , chapter 3 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Complex+cobordism+and+stable+homotopy+groups+of+spheres"} Complex cobordism and stable homotopy groups of spheres
            %li#Kochman96
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stanley+Kochman"}> Stanley Kochman
                , chapter 5 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bordism%2C+Stable+Homotopy+and+Adams+Spectral+Sequences"} Bordism, Stable Homotopy and Adams Spectral Sequences
          %p Further review includes
          %ul
            %li#MosherTangora68
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Mosher"}> Robert Mosher
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Tangora"} Martin Tangora
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Cohomology Operations and Application in Homotopy Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~aar/papers/moshtang.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+McCleary"}> John McCleary
                , chapter 9 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A User’s Guide to Spectral Sequences
            %li#Bruner09
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Bruner"}> Robert Bruner
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An Adams spectral sequence primer
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.wayne.edu/~rrb/papers/adams.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Rognes12
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Rognes"}> John Rognes
                ,
                %em The Adams spectral sequence
                (following
                = succeed ")," do
